    "report": "# Research-Gap Report: Federated Learning\n\n## Topic Landscape\n\nThe federated learning literature clusters into seventeen identifiable themes spanning foundational and applied concerns. The largest and most established cluster is a broad \"data / research / challenges\" body (31 papers, mean year 2021.3), reflecting the field's ongoing engagement with core methodological questions. Close behind are active threads on IoT-based intrusion detection (22 papers, mean year 2022.5), personalization and local model adaptation (21 papers, 2021.0), data heterogeneity (18 papers, 2021.2), and blockchain-based privacy (18 papers, 2021.5). Security themes\u2014backdoor attacks, gradient poisoning, and trust\u2014appear across multiple clusters with mean years ranging from 2020.8 to 2022.2, signaling sustained and growing concern. Applied domains are represented by healthcare/medical imaging (14 and 9 papers respectively, mean years 2021.9\u20132022.9) and a small but notably recent cluster on CNN-based crop/leaf disease detection (7 papers, mean year 2023.0). Emerging topics include federated LLMs (8 papers, 2022.4) and knowledge distillation (7 papers, 2021.9), suggesting the field is beginning to expand toward large-model and continual-learning paradigms.\n\n---\n\n## Validated Gap\n\n### Gap: Federated Intrusion Detection for Agricultural IoT and Crop Disease Monitoring\n\n**Why it is a gap:**\nThe IoT/intrusion-detection cluster (22 papers, mean year 2022.5) and the crop/leaf disease CNN cluster (7 papers, mean year 2023.0) are bridged by 159 papers that touch both domains individually, yet a targeted search for their intersection\u2014using queries `\"intrusion detection\" \"crop disease\"` and `\"IoT detection\" \"leaf disease\"`\u2014returned only **1 recent paper**. The validated prominence score of **0.11** confirms that despite substantial activity on either side, the cross-domain synthesis is almost entirely absent. The confidence in this gap is **0.96**, the highest possible tier in the validation pipeline.\n\nConcretely, the gap is this: agricultural IoT deployments increasingly use edge/FL-based CNN models to detect plant diseases in real time, yet the security of these pipelines\u2014specifically, whether federated intrusion-detection mechanisms can protect the integrity of crop-disease inference systems from adversarial or anomalous network traffic\u2014has not been studied. The two communities have developed in parallel without cross-pollination.\n\n**Research Agenda:**\n\n1. **Threat characterization and dataset construction.** Profile the attack surface of federated agricultural IoT networks (sensor spoofing, gradient poisoning, model hijacking) and construct a labeled benchmark dataset that couples network intrusion events with concurrent crop-disease inference tasks, enabling joint evaluation.\n\n2. **Co-design of FL intrusion detection and disease inference.** Develop a unified federated architecture in which a shared local model simultaneously performs CNN-based leaf-disease classification and anomaly/intrusion detection on incoming IoT traffic, leveraging multi-task learning to share representations while preserving privacy via differential privacy or secure aggregation.\n\n3. **Field validation and robustness testing.** Deploy the co-designed system in a realistic agricultural edge environment (e.g., greenhouse or orchard IoT testbed), measure the trade-off between detection accuracy for both tasks under non-IID data conditions, and evaluate resilience against adaptive adversaries who exploit knowledge of the dual-task structure.\n\n---\n\n## Rejected Candidates\n\nSeven candidate gaps\u2014including IoT \u00d7 general FL challenges (12,973 recent papers), blockchain \u00d7 privacy challenges (2,645), and edge devices \u00d7 FL challenges (3,623)\u2014were rejected because literature search returned hundreds to tens of thousands of recent papers, confirming these intersections are already well-covered by the research community."
